                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A fun little game with an incredibly moving ending! The puzzle design was genius, new mechanics for each area were introduced in a way that felt both intuitive and fun. I never once felt like the game was holding my hand when it came to each level's challenges. Each mechanic was unique and built upon each other in very fun ways. The story is very much like a visual novel, with the player having little to no agency. So many NPC stories felt like there should be more to explore/discover. Sadly, if there was a way to do so, I wasn't able to figure out how. Any actions you take don't impact the world/plot in any meaningful way beyond what was predestined by the devs. Not to say this is a negative! From the beginning, Even the Ocean was presented as a story, with the first character you meet being named the \"storyteller.\" If you enjoy that sort of experience, the story was quite well written! If you don't, there is a mode where you can just play the puzzles. My only disappointment was that the world felt like it had so much more to offer than what was given to us. The art design was amazing, with every you place you explore having its own fun, unique vibe. \n\nTLDR: Even the Ocean was a very fun and beautiful puzzle platformer with a moving story, but a sad lack of side stories and area depth. I was left wanting so much more!",
